{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "reached_index",
      "description": "During retracing, a motion can finish just outside the strict arrival tolerance and the next motion can show completion of that endpoint followed by arrival at its successor. The carried index can therefore lag by two endpoints: advancing it only once leaves progress behind the observed trace. This breaks PREDICTABILITY and subsequently SUFFICIENCY. Keep this field as the zero-based index of the latest consecutively confirmed endpoint, initialized to 0. At each cycle, scan the observed motion chronologically and advance through every consecutive endpoint whose arrival is established by stick position within 1 cm and supporting endpoint activation. Do not impose a one-increment-per-cycle limit. Retain the strict confirmation criterion: a near-arrival outside 1 cm does not itself change this field, even if the action proceeds onward. For example, an incoming value of 4 becomes 6 when the motion shows confirmation of pattern[5] followed by pattern[6]. Preserve the value when no steps execute.",
      "value_type": "count"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "reached_index",
      "description": "During retracing, the incoming index may still precede an endpoint that the previous action treated as sufficiently close to proceed. The current motion frames can then show that endpoint activating and the stick reaching the following endpoint. Incrementing at most once leaves the memory behind, breaking PREDICTABILITY and causing an already reached waypoint to be commanded again. Keep reached_index as the latest consecutively confirmed endpoint, not the last destination commanded. Initialize it to 0 as before. Starting from the incoming index, scan this cycle's motion frames in chronological order, using the current wrist image and TCP pose as additional evidence. Each time arrival at the next listed node is established within 1 cm with supporting endpoint activation, advance the working index and continue checking subsequent frames for the following node. Write the final working index; for example, write 6 from incoming 4 when two consecutive arrivals are shown. Mere proximity outside 1 cm or choosing an onward action does not advance this field. If no steps executed, retain it."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "While retracing, the stick can finish a segment only slightly outside the strict memory-confirmation radius: an observed residual of about 10.23 mm led the policy to command a corrective return, although the correct action continued to the next demonstrated endpoint. After that continuation, motion frames can confirm two consecutive arrivals; considering only one made the policy command an endpoint already reached. These are SUFFICIENCY failures. Replace the rule that action progress must always equal the strictly confirmed memory index with two explicit cases. First compute the confirmed index by scanning all this cycle's frames for consecutive arrivals, without a one-arrival limit, using the revised memory rule. Normally target the node immediately after that index. However, when steps have executed and the current TCP is within 0.012 m of that next node's ideal waypoint, with the observed motion consistent with finishing the segment to it, treat that node as complete for action selection and target its successor rather than correcting the small residual. This action-only near-arrival allowance does not change reached_index unless strict confirmation is also observed, and does not permit skipping any other unconfirmed node. Remove the unconditional instruction to retry every endpoint not yet strictly confirmed; retry only when neither strict confirmation nor this near-arrival case applies. Keep ideal grid coordinates, z=0.070 m, approximately downward orientation, direct tracing, and the existing no-step handling. When the near-arrival node is the final node, use the existing single-step success-evaluation wait rather than inventing a further tracing waypoint."
}

